@page "/expivot/live"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExPivot" Title="Live data"
          Lead="Records that change while the report is read: each change makes the next Snapshot, ExPivot asks again, and the values that moved are marked for a moment.">

    <DocsSection Title="Change Batches">
        <p>
            A source over records with a Record Key takes <strong>Change Batches</strong>: the records added, the
            records changed (found by their key) and the keys removed. <code>Sales.Fields.Batch(added, changed,
            removedKeys)</code> reads one through the same declarations, and <code>SnapshotPivotSource.Apply</code>
            applies it.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Whole or not at all.</strong> The Snapshot makes the next one, sharing everything the batch did
                not touch. A batch that changes a key the Snapshot does not hold, adds one it does, or names a key twice
                is refused by name, and nothing of it is applied.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Folded in, not read again.</strong> The answer held for the current question is brought up to
                date from what the batch removed and added. Every leaf still equals a fresh aggregation of the new
                Snapshot, to the last bit.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>ExPivot listens to <code>Changed</code></strong> and asks again. Changes are gathered and the report
                is redrawn at most every <code>RedrawInterval</code> (250 ms by default; zero redraws on every change).
                A change of values keeps the Selection and any open menu or panel.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(LiveBatches)" Mud="typeof(LiveBatchesMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Five sales are amended every 400 ms. Select a few cells, or open a field's menu: they stay as the values move.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Reading a changing report">
        <p>
            ExPivot's report is display-only. ExGrid's rule that values changing alone do not
            refuse a user's write adds no write-back operation to a pivot report. Copy and Summary
            still name the Report Version the user acted on, and Show Details the Source Version
            its records add up under. If that version is no longer available, the source refuses
            by name instead of silently returning newer numbers. These read operations and the
            timed Change Highlight keep their own version evidence as the report changes.
        </p>
        <p>
            A refused Copy is said through the grid's live region, and a declined Summary in place of its
            figures, in ExPivot's own words, which <code>Label</code> replaces by id and
            <code>PivotWords.Japanese</code> gives in Japanese, as every word ExPivot shows. A source's own
            sentence is set inside <code>source-refused</code> ("The source refused to answer: …"). An answer
            for another Report Version, or one that leaves a selected range out or cuts it short, is refused
            too (<code>copy-another-version</code>, <code>copy-missing-range</code>,
            <code>copy-incomplete-range</code>, <code>summary-another-version</code>,
            <code>summary-inconsistent-errors</code>), never written to the clipboard or summed.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The Change Highlight">
        <p>
            A value cell whose painted text changed with the data is marked for <code>ChangeHighlightDuration</code>,
            one second by default; zero marks nothing. Only data marks a cell: a Change Batch, a server's notice,
            Refresh or a new source. A new layout, a sort, a collapse or Show Values As marks nothing, and neither does a
            change the number format hides. The mark's colour is the Visual Token
            <code>--ex-change-highlight-background</code>, which <code>ExGrid.MudBlazor</code>'s stylesheet maps to the theme.
        </p>
        <p>
            The time a mark starts is ExPivot's own, on its <code>Clock</code>. A report source says which cells
            changed in which Report Version, never when; ExPivot starts the mark when it first shows that version,
            so a server whose clock is ahead of the browser's, or behind it, changes nothing on screen. A changed
            row first scrolled into view a moment later is marked as of when the change was first shown, and its
            mark ends with the others.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A server whose data moves on">
        <p>
            A server's source learns of changes however your application does: SignalR, polling, a message bus. Pass
            each notice to <code>FetchingPivotReportSource.NotifyChanged(version)</code>, and ExPivot asks the server again for
            the current report Window, as Window Changes once one is on screen. A <em>Refresh</em> on the Pivot Toolbar does the same when the source says it can be
            refreshed.
        </p>
        <p>
            When the newest data cannot be shown (it breaks a cap, the source fails, a Refresh fails, or memory runs out
            while its report is laid out), the report stays on the last version it could compute, and a notice under the
            Pivot Toolbar says what happened and as of when, with Retry. The report never shows half a change. Memory running
            out is said as such, not as the source's failure: in WebAssembly the heap does not shrink again, so the next change
            asks again rather than assume it will fit. A layout you ask for that runs out of memory is refused instead, and the
            Field List goes back to the layout the report shows.
        </p>
        <p>
            While the report is stale, its value cells are painted in a muted colour too, so a reader who has not looked at the
            notice still cannot take the numbers for the newest. They stay readable, and nothing animates; the labels keep
            their colour, and the mark goes with the notice when the report recovers. The colour is the Visual Token
            <code>--ex-pivot-stale-value-color</code>: unset, ExPivot mixes the grid's text into its ground, which keeps a
            readable contrast in either scheme, and <code>ExPivot.MudBlazor</code>'s stylesheet maps it to the palette's
            secondary text.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(LiveServer)" Mud="typeof(LiveServerMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesServer.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            The simulated server amends eight sales a second and announces each new Source Version. Cut it off: the next
            question fails, and the report stays as it was, stale, with its notice and its numbers muted, until you reconnect
            and press Retry.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
